= Implementation notes

== Rendering milestone
- PRD-002 keeps rendering example-scoped: generate a reviewable Typst assembly from the parsed publication, then delegate PDF and HTML export to Typst libraries. Correct approach for this milestone is `typst-pdf` and the matching HTML exporter crate from Rust, not invoking the Typst CLI, query-engine evaluation, or final routed pages.
- PRD-002 review follow-up: the assembly artifact is a reviewable intermediate, not a requirement to hand-build Typst with strings. Correct approach is to preserve authored source content for reachable nodes, use Typst syntax/AST machinery where it helps, replace unimplemented projection output with explicit placeholders, and still persist the generated assembly so PDF and HTML exports share the same input.
- PRD-002 discussion correction: string-stitching should be treated as the wrong implementation model, not merely a less preferred one. Correct approach: build a structural assembly from Typst syntax/AST nodes or a narrow typed render IR, then serialize `.typ` only as an output artifact.
- PRD-002 export slice: attempted `cargo fetch` after adding `typst-pdf` in the sandbox first; it failed DNS resolution for `index.crates.io`. Correct approach: rerun the same `cargo fetch` with network approval, then keep `typst`, `typst-html`, `typst-pdf`, `typst-kit`, and `typst-syntax` on the aligned `0.14.2` Typst stack.
- PRD-002 export slice: first `cargo run --example api_sketch` failed at HTML compilation with `html export is only available when --features html is passed`. Correct approach for library use is to build the Typst `Library` with `Features::from_iter([Feature::Html])` in the render world before compiling `typst_html::HtmlDocument`.
- PRD-002 HTML correction: the single `api-sketch.html` inspect-style review document is the wrong target. Correct approach: keep `api-sketch.typ` and `api-sketch.pdf` as combined proof artifacts if useful, but compile one HTML document per reachable source node from transformed authored Typst source. Projection calls are replaced in-place with explicit placeholders for now; the current replacement is intentionally narrow to canonical `#publisher.outline`, `#publisher.bibliography`, `#publisher.ref`, and `#publisher.nav.suppress` calls, not alias or wrapper expansion.
- PRD-002 per-node HTML attempt: first focused render test failed because visible `@cite` placeholder text was still parsed as a Typst label reference, and the fixture PNG bytes failed HTML image decoding. Correct approach: render shorthand reference placeholders through `raw` text so Typst does not resolve them, and have the HTML render world return a valid placeholder image for image assets.
- PRD-002 inherited navigation correction: rendering only placeholders found at authored canonical calls missed inherited navigation projections on pages such as `writing.html`. Correct approach: emit remaining navigation projections as page-level placeholders before authored content, while still replacing authored projection calls in place.
- PRD-002 PDF/source correction: the old combined `api-sketch.typ` was an inspect-style assembly that diverged from per-node HTML. Correct approach: write generated per-node `.typ` files under `build/api-sketch/typst/`, compile each HTML page from its corresponding `.typ` file, and make `api-sketch.typ` a single-document PDF source that includes those same page `.typ` files with page breaks.
- PRD-002 regression assertion correction: first tightened test expected HTML quotes to be entity-encoded as `&quot;`, but Typst HTML emitted literal quotes inside the code block. Correct approach: assert the stable rendered text `scope="nav:index"` rather than an encoding form that the exporter does not produce here.

== Core entities inspect
- Attempted `cargo test --test inspect_api_sketch` before any accepted `insta` snapshot existed. It compiled and produced `tests/snapshots/inspect_api_sketch__inspect_api_sketch_snapshot_is_stable.snap.new`, then failed because the snapshot was unreviewed. Correct approach: accept the generated snapshot with `INSTA_UPDATE=always cargo test --test inspect_api_sketch`, then rerun the focused test normally.
- PR #57 review follow-up: `docs/api-sketch.typ` is an aspirational API sketch, so it can show the intended `#let nav = publisher.scope(...)` / `#nav.suppress()` shape even while the milestone parser only implements `publisher.nav.suppress()`. Parser call dispatch now routes exact `publisher.*` calls through a `PublisherCallHandler` trait boundary instead of a widening `match` over every supported call.
- PR #58 marker evaluation: attempted `cargo fetch` for embedded Typst dependencies inside the sandbox first; it failed DNS resolution for `index.crates.io`. Correct approach: rerun the same Cargo fetch with network approval, then keep `typst-kit` on `default-features = false` with `embed-fonts` only.
- PR #58 marker evaluation: tried representing `publisher.nav` as a Typst dictionary with a `suppress` function. Typst reported that dictionaries do not have a `suppress` method for `publisher.nav.suppress()`. Correct approach: put `suppress` in a small imported module and alias that module as `nav` from `publisher.typ`.
- PR #58 marker evaluation: full Typst evaluation initially made parser extraction depend on ordinary document rendering details, including invalid image fixture bytes and unresolved `@cite` references. Correct approach: use a parser-only Typst world that returns placeholder bytes for image files and sanitizes reference shorthand during marker evaluation while `typst-syntax` still extracts citations from the original source.
- PR #58 marker evaluation: imported support files such as `publisher.typ` were first reported as unreachable Typst source files. Correct approach: track Typst source imports during marker evaluation and exclude local publisher support files from publication-node reachability warnings.

== Selector/scope discovery
- PRD-003 fixture check: directly compiling nested source files such as `examples/prd003_discovery_site/writing/blog-2.typ` first failed with Typst access denied for `../publisher.typ`, because the CLI default project root is the source file directory. Correct approach for nested fixture checks is `typst compile --root examples/prd003_discovery_site ...`.
- PRD-003 HTML fixture check: `typst compile --format html ...` first failed because HTML export is feature-gated. Correct CLI approach is to pass `--features html`; Rust library worlds must build the `Library` with `Features::from_iter([Feature::Html])`.
- PRD-003 source-local reference check: compiling `writing/blog-2.typ` with the fixture root still failed because `@thesis-main` is outside that compiled source document. Current conclusion: ordinary Typst references need the target label in the compiled document; per-source HTML with cross-source references needs a publisher adapter such as generated reference/link stubs or another assembly strategy.
- PRD-003 discovery harness: first `cargo run --example prd003_discovery` failed because the local `CompileFailure` type did not implement `std::error::Error`, and `HeadingElem` level access was attempted through a field accessor without a `StyleChain`. Correct API approach is to implement `Display`/`Error` for the harness error and use `HeadingElem::resolve_level(StyleChain::default())` when inspecting heading levels.
- PRD-003 discovery harness: the next run built but failed at runtime searching for `/Users/jasoncairns/Development/my-site/index.typ`; graph entries are fixture-relative, while the Typst world expects repo-relative paths. Correct approach is to prefix fixture graph entries with `examples/prd003_discovery_site/` before compiling or introspecting them.
- PRD-003 selector probe: trying to use a `block` or `context` as a lexical query boundary still let `query(heading)` see headings outside the block. Correct conclusion: use the compiled Typst document as the default rendered-region boundary, or use explicit selectors such as `selector(heading).after(...).before(...)` for contiguous marker-bounded windows.
- PRD-003 reference probe: hidden-including a target source made an external label resolve, but the hidden heading and citations still polluted ordinary introspection and bibliography inputs. Correct conclusion: per-source HTML cross-source references need a real reference/link adapter, not hidden target inclusion.
- PRD-003 heading-promotion probe: a broad show-rule re-emitting headings hit maximum show-rule depth, and a narrowed rule produced both original and generated heading levels. Correct conclusion: scope heading promotion should be generated-source or AST work at the render entrypoint, not a Typst show-rule trick.
- PRD-003 counter probe: compiling `thesis/ch-01.typ` and `thesis/ch-02.typ` through one generated entrypoint rendered headings as `1.` and `2.` naturally, while compiling `ch-02.typ` alone rendered it as `1.`. Correct approach for cross-source numbering in per-source HTML is to provide counter seed/state at the start of the generated source, e.g. `#counter(heading).update(1)` before the second top-level heading so it renders as `2.` and nested headings as `2.1.`.
- PRD-003 reference probe: compiling a combined document containing `ch-01`, `ch-02`, and a blog reference rendered `@ch-02` as a normal link to `#ch-02` with display `Section 2`, while compiling the blog alone failed with missing label. Correct conclusion: scope/edition entrypoints can use native references when targets are present; per-source HTML needs a generated link/display adapter for external-source references.
- PRD-003 marker slice: first `cargo fmt --check` after adding focused parser assertions failed only because the new assertion wrapping was not rustfmt-formatted. Correct approach is to run `cargo fmt` after edits, then rerun `cargo fmt --check` before validation.
- PRD-003 scope validation slice: first `cargo fmt --check` after adding active-scope assertions failed only because the new assertion wrapping was not rustfmt-formatted. Correct approach remains to run `cargo fmt`, then rerun the focused test and `cargo fmt --check`.
